      "title": "Layer 2 Mental Model: Frames, MACs and Switches",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Layer 2 Mental Model: Frames, MACs and Switches",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. The Ethernet Frame",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Dst MAC",
                    "sub": "6B",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Src MAC",
                    "sub": "6B",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "EtherType",
                    "sub": "2B",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Payload",
                    "sub": "46-1500B",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "FCS",
                    "sub": "4B",
                    "ink": "red"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==MTU 1500== is the **payload** limit, not the whole frame",
                "EtherType says what the payload is:"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "EtherType",
                "Payload"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`0x0800`",
                  "IPv4"
                ],
                [
                  "`0x0806`",
                  "ARP"
                ],
                [
                  "`0x86DD`",
                  "IPv6"
                ],
                [
                  "`0x8100`",
                  "802.1Q VLAN tag"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. MAC vs IP",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==MAC== = 48 bits in hex: `AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF`",
                "First 24 bits = ==OUI== (vendor); last 24 = device-specific",
                "Broadcast = `FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF`",
                "MAC: Layer 2, one segment only. IP: Layer 3, end to end"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Every router hop",
              "text": "The ==MAC pair is rewritten== at each hop. The **IP pair** stays the same."
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Hub vs Switch",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Hub",
                "Switch"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Forwards",
                  "every frame out all ports",
                  "by destination MAC"
                ],
                [
                  "Collision domains",
                  "one for all",
                  "one per port"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "How a Switch Builds and Uses Its MAC Table",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "How a Switch Builds and Uses Its MAC Table",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Learn, Then Forward",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Frame in",
                    "sub": "on a port",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Learn",
                    "sub": "src MAC → ingress port",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Lookup",
                    "sub": "dst MAC",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Send",
                    "sub": "one port or flood",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Frame",
                "Action"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Known unicast",
                  "Out that **one port** only"
                ],
                [
                  "Unknown unicast",
                  "{orange:Flood} every VLAN port except ingress"
                ],
                [
                  "Broadcast",
                  "{orange:Flood} every VLAN port except ingress"
                ],
                [
                  "Any frame",
                  "Never sent back out its ==ingress port=="
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Aging and Moves",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Idle entry ages out after ==300 s== (Cisco default)",
                "Each new frame from that source **refreshes** the timer",
                "A host that moves ports is ==relearned== from its next frame"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Example: A to B",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "chain",
              "spec": {
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "text": "A → B, table empty",
                    "note": "learn A on port 1; B unknown, flood",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "B replies to A",
                    "note": "learn B on port 2; A known, out port 1",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "A → B again",
                    "note": "B known: port 2 only",
                    "ink": "green"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "4. Mistake",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Source only",
              "text": "The switch never learns from the ==destination== MAC. Broadcast FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF can never be a source, so it is **never** in the table."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "ARP: Resolving IP to MAC",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "ARP: Resolving IP to MAC",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What ARP Does",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==ARP== maps a known next-hop **IPv4** address to a **MAC** on the same subnet",
                "Runs between Layer 2 and Layer 3",
                "Carried directly in Ethernet: EtherType `0x0806`"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Request, Reply and Send Flow",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Request",
                "Reply"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Sent to",
                  "Broadcast `FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF`",
                  "Unicast to the asker"
                ],
                [
                  "Says",
                  "Who has 10.0.0.5? Tell 10.0.0.2",
                  "10.0.0.5 is at AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 0
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flowchart",
              "spec": {
                "title": "Which IP do I ARP for?",
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "q1"
                  ],
                  [
                    "a",
                    "b"
                  ]
                ],
                "nodes": {
                  "q1": {
                    "text": "Destination on my subnet?",
                    "shape": "diamond"
                  },
                  "a": {
                    "text": "ARP for the destination IP",
                    "shape": "box",
                    "ink": "green"
                  },
                  "b": {
                    "text": "ARP for the gateway IP, send frame to gateway MAC",
                    "shape": "box",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  }
                },
                "edges": [
                  {
                    "from": "q1",
                    "to": "a",
                    "label": "yes"
                  },
                  {
                    "from": "q1",
                    "to": "b",
                    "label": "no"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Cache and Spoofing",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "ARP cache keeps IP → MAC for ==minutes== (OS dependent): no new request per packet",
                "Receiver also caches the **sender's** mapping from the request",
                "{blue:Gratuitous ARP}: host announces its own IP → MAC, refreshing caches and detecting duplicate IPs",
                "No authentication: ARP spoofing can ==poison== caches"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Defence",
              "text": "**Dynamic ARP Inspection** on the switch drops forged ARP replies."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "ARP and Switch Commands in Practice",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "ARP and Switch Commands in Practice",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Host Commands",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Goal",
                "Command"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Show ARP cache",
                  "`arp -a` or `ip neigh show`"
                ],
                [
                  "Clear one entry",
                  "`arp -d <ip>`"
                ],
                [
                  "Clear all (Linux)",
                  "`sudo ip neigh flush all`"
                ],
                [
                  "Static entry",
                  "`arp -s 10.0.0.5 aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff` (Windows: `aa-bb-cc-dd-ee-ff`)"
                ],
                [
                  "Own MAC (Linux)",
                  "`ip link show`"
                ],
                [
                  "Own MAC (Windows)",
                  "`ipconfig /all`"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{green:REACHABLE}: recently confirmed",
                "{orange:STALE}: aged, re-checked on next use",
                "{red:FAILED}: no reply to the ARP request"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Clear and Pin Entries",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Wrong or stale entry: ==delete it==, the next packet re-ARPs",
                "Static entry is ==permanent==: it survives aging",
                "Static entries must be ==maintained by hand=="
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Cisco MAC Table",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`show mac address-table` lists ==VLAN==, ==MAC==, ==type== and ==port==",
                "`clear mac address-table dynamic` flushes learned entries"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "text",
              "lines": [
                "show mac address-table",
                "clear mac address-table dynamic"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "output",
              "lines": [
                "Vlan  Mac Address       Type     Ports",
                "10    aaaa.bbbb.cccc    DYNAMIC  Gi0/1"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "`arp -d <ip>` removes a host entry, static ones too. `clear mac address-table dynamic` clears learned switch entries and leaves static ones."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "VLANs, Trunking and Broadcast Domains",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "VLANs, Trunking and Broadcast Domains",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Broadcast Domains and VLANs",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Broadcast domain== = every device that gets a Layer 2 broadcast",
                "Switches do {red:not} split it; ==routers== do",
                "A ==VLAN== splits one switch into separate L2 networks",
                "Each VLAN = its own broadcast domain",
                "Different VLANs need a Layer 3 device: router or SVI"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Access vs Trunk",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Access",
                "Trunk"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "VLANs",
                  "exactly one",
                  "many"
                ],
                [
                  "Frames",
                  "untagged",
                  "802.1Q tagged"
                ],
                [
                  "Links",
                  "hosts",
                  "switch to switch"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "802.1Q tag: ==4 bytes==, 12-bit VLAN ID, range ==1-4094==",
                "==Native VLAN== crosses a trunk untagged (default VLAN 1)",
                "Native VLAN mismatch leaks traffic and warns: match both ends, move off VLAN 1"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Config",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "text",
              "lines": [
                "interface g0/1",
                " switchport mode access",
                " switchport access vlan 10",
                "interface g0/24",
                " switchport mode trunk",
                " switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==One VLAN per subnet==",
                "Inter-VLAN routing: ==router-on-a-stick== or Layer 3 switch"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Switches never stop broadcasts; different VLANs cannot ping without routing"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
